   1→# VEX Corpus: Channel References
   2→
   3→> 169 examples from vex-corpus. Sources: cgwiki-vex, joy-of-vex-youtube, sidefx-vex-reference, vex-corpus-blueprints
   4→
   5→## Beginner (13 examples)
   6→
   7→### Normal Displacement with Channels
   8→
   9→```vex
  10→@P += @N * ch('push');
  11→```
  12→
  13→Displacing points along their normals automatically updates the normal vectors when smooth shading is applied.
  14→
  15→### Vector Addition Basics
  16→
  17→```vex
  18→vector a = chv('a');
  19→vector b = chv('b');
  20→@P = a+b;
  21→```
  22→
  23→Demonstrates basic vector addition by creating two vector parameters and adding them together to set the point position.
  24→
  25→### Vector Addition Basics
  26→
  27→```vex
  28→vector a = chv('a');
  29→vector b = chv('b');
  30→@N = a + b;
  31→```
  32→
  33→Demonstrates basic vector addition by creating two vector parameters and adding them together, storing the result in the normal attribute.
  34→
  35→### Vector Addition Basics
  36→
  37→```vex
  38→vector a = chv('a');
  39→vector b = chv('b');
  40→@P = a + b;
  41→```
  42→
  43→Demonstrates basic vector addition by reading two vector parameters from channel references and setting the point position to their sum.
  44→
  45→### Adding Vector to Position
  46→
  47→```vex
  48→vector a = chv('a');
  49→@P += a;
  50→```
  51→
  52→Reads a vector parameter from a UI channel and adds it to the point position.
  53→
  54→### Setting Normals with Channel Reference
  55→
  56→```vex
  57→@N = chf('scale');
  58→```
  59→
  60→Sets the normal vector (@N) to a uniform scalar value controlled by a channel parameter named 'scale'.
  61→
  62→### Scaling Normals with Channel Reference
  63→
  64→```vex
  65→@N *= ch('scale');
  66→```
  67→
  68→Multiplies the normal vector by a channel parameter named 'scale', allowing interactive control over normal magnitude for effects like explosions or directional displacement.
  69→
  70→### Scaling Normals with Channel
  71→
  72→```vex
  73→v@N * ch('scale');
  74→```
  75→
  76→Multiplies the normal vector by a channel parameter value, demonstrating vector multiplication with scalar values from channel references.
  77→
  78→### Multiplication with Channel Reference
  79→
  80→```vex
  81→@P *= ch('scale');
  82→```
  83→
  84→Multiplies the position of each point by a channel parameter named 'scale', effectively scaling the geometry uniformly.
  85→
  86→### Vector Scaling with Parameters
  87→
  88→```vex
  89→*= ch('scale');
  90→```
  91→
  92→Uses the compound assignment operator to scale a vector attribute by a channel parameter value.
  93→
  94→### Vector-Vector Multiplication with chv()
  95→
  96→```vex
  97→@N *= chv('scalevec');
  98→```
  99→
 100→Demonstrates multiplying a normal vector by a vector channel parameter using chv().
 101→
 102→### Setting pscale from channel
 103→
 104→```vex
 105→@pscale = ch('pscale');
 106→```
 107→
 108→Sets the pscale attribute for each point by reading a parameter value from a channel reference.
 109→
 110→### Adding Vector to Normal
 111→
 112→```vex
 113→vector a = chv('a');
 114→@N += a;
 115→```
 116→
 117→Creates a vector variable from a vector channel parameter, then adds that vector to the point normal attribute using the compound assignment operator.
 118→
 119→## Intermediate (147 examples)
 120→
 121→### Create UI controls â
 122→
 123→```vex
 124→@Cd.r = sin( @P.x *  5  );
 125→```
 126→
 127→Say you have this:
 128→
 129→But you want to change 5 to another number.
 130→
 131→### Example: Wave deformer â
 132→
 133→```vex
 134→@d = length(@P);
 135→```
 136→
 137→A classic.
 138→
 139→### Attributes vs variables â
 140→
 141→```vex
 142→float foo;
 143→vector bar = {0,0,0};
 144→matrix m;
 145→
 146→foo = 8;
 147→bar.x += foo;
 148→```
 149→
 150→The examples given above all use the @ syntax to get and set attributes on points.
 151→
 152→### Generate a disk of vectors perpendicular to a vector â
 153→
 154→```vex
 155→vector aim;
 156→vector4 q;
 157→
 158→aim = chv('aim');
 159→@N = sample_circle_edge_uniform(rand(@ptnum));
 160→q = dihedral({0,0,1}, aim);
 161→@N = qrotate(q,@N);
 162→@N *= ch('scale');
 163→```
 164→
 165→Download scene: Download file: disk_around_vector.hip
 166→
 167→My first H16 demo scene, hooray!
 168→
 169→Using another of the super handy sample_something_something functions, sample_circle_edge_uniform generates ....
 170→
 171→### Distance â
 172→
 173→```vex
 174→float d = distance(@P, {1,0,3} );
 175→ d *= ch('scale');
 176→ @Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 177→```
 178→
 179→Related to length is distance .
 180→
 181→### Minpos â
 182→
 183→```vex
 184→vector pos = minpos(1,@P);
 185→ float d = distance(@P, pos);
 186→ d *= ch('scale');
 187→ @Cd = 0;
 188→ @Cd.r = sin(d);
 189→```
 190→
 191→You might have used the ray sop before, minpos is the vex equivalent of that; give it a position, and some geometry, it will return the closest position on the surface of that geometry.
 192→
 193→### Dot product â
 194→
 195→```vex
 196→@Cd = @N.y;
 197→```
 198→
 199→Switch to a more complex model now, like tommy, make sure his textures are disabled and 'show uv texture' in the viewport is disabled so we're not distracted by those.
 200→
 201→That looks like he's lit fro....
 202→
 203→### Vector addition â
 204→
 205→```vex
 206→vector a = chv('a');
 207→vector b = chv('b');
 208→@N = a+b;
 209→```
 210→
 211→The maths textbook definition of adding vectors is to lie the vectors tip-to-tail, then draw a new vector from the start of the first to the end of the last.
 212→
 213→### Vector multiplication â
 214→
 215→```vex
 216→@N *= ch('scale');
 217→```
 218→
 219→Multiplying a vector by a single number just multiplies each number inside the vector by the single number.
 220→
 221→### Distance-based color patterns with center control
 222→
 223→```vex
 224→float d = length(@P) * ch('scale');
 225→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 226→
 227→@Cd = (sin(length(@P) * ch('scale'))+1)*0.5;
 228→
 229→float d = distance(@P, {1,0,1});
 230→d *= ch('scale');
 231→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 232→// ...
 233→```
 234→
 235→Progression from using length() to measure distance from origin to using distance() function to measure from arbitrary points in space.
 236→
 237→### Vector scaling in distance calculations
 238→
 239→```vex
 240→vector center = chv('center');
 241→float d = distance(@P * {0.5,1,1}, center);
 242→d *= ch('scale');
 243→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 244→
 245→// Alternative versions:
 246→// float d = distance(@P, {1,0,1});
 247→// d *= ch('scale');
 248→// ...
 249→```
 250→
 251→Demonstrates multiplying the position vector by another vector to scale individual components before computing distance.
 252→
 253→### Using fit() with sin() for animation
 254→
 255→```vex
 256→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 257→vector center = chv('center');
 258→float d = distance(pos, center);
 259→d *= ch('scale');
 260→@Cd = fit(d);
 261→
 262→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 263→vector center = chv('center');
 264→// ...
 265→```
 266→
 267→Demonstrates the progression of using fit() to remap values, starting with incomplete fit() calls, then properly using fit() to remap sin(d) from its natural range of -1 to 1 into the 0 to 1 range ....
 268→
 269→### Animating patterns with @Time
 270→
 271→```vex
 272→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 273→vector center = chv('center');
 274→float d = distance(pos, center);
 275→d *= ch('scale');
 276→@Cd = fit(sin(d), -1, 1, 0, 1);
 277→
 278→// Animated version:
 279→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 280→// ...
 281→```
 282→
 283→Demonstrates animating a radial sine wave pattern by adding the @Time attribute to the distance calculation.
 284→
 285→### Using fit() with animation
 286→
 287→```vex
 288→vector pos = @P * chv("fancyscale");
 289→vector center = chv("center");
 290→float d = distance(pos, center);
 291→d *= ch("scale");
 292→@Cd = fit(sin(d), -1, 1, 0, 1);
 293→
 294→// Animated version
 295→vector pos = @P * chv("fancyscale");
 296→// ...
 297→```
 298→
 299→Demonstrates using the fit() function to map sine wave patterns to color values, creating radial color gradients from a center point.
 300→
 301→### VEX Code Style Conventions
 302→
 303→```vex
 304→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 305→vector center = chv('center');
 306→float d = distance(pos, center);
 307→@Cd = ch('scale');
 308→@Cd = fit(sin(d*@Time), -1, 1, 0, 1);
 309→
 310→foo = foo * 5;
 311→foo *= 5;
 312→// ...
 313→```
 314→
 315→Demonstrates different VEX code style conventions and operator shorthand syntax.
 316→
 317→### Channel References and Art Directability
 318→
 319→```vex
 320→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 321→vector center = chv('center');
 322→float d = distance(pos, center);
 323→@Cd = ch('scale');
 324→@Cd = fit(sin(d*@Frame), -1, 1, 0, 1);
 325→
 326→foo *= 5;
 327→
 328→// ...
 329→```
 330→
 331→Demonstrates using channel references (ch() and chv()) to create art-directable VEX effects by exposing parameters that can be animated or adjusted interactively.
 332→
 333→### Code Style and Mathematical Operations
 334→
 335→```vex
 336→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 337→vector center = chv('center');
 338→float d = distance(pos, center);
 339→d *= ch('scale');
 340→@Cd = fit(sin(d), -1, 1, 0, 1);
 341→
 342→foo -= 5;
 343→foo *= 5;
 344→// ...
 345→```
 346→
 347→Demonstrates VEX code styling conventions and compound assignment operators for building complex mathematical expressions.
 348→
 349→### Multi-line Code Organization
 350→
 351→```vex
 352→float foo = 1;
 353→
 354→vector pos = set(0, sin(@Time), 0);
 355→vector center = chv('center');
 356→float d = distance(pos, center);
 357→d *= ch('scale');
 358→@Cd = fit(sin(d * chf('time')), -1, 1, 0, 1);
 359→
 360→// ...
 361→```
 362→
 363→Breaking complex calculations into multiple lines with descriptive comments makes code easier to understand and debug.
 364→
 365→### Position and Normal Manipulation
 366→
 367→```vex
 368→vector pos = v@P * exp(-length(v@P));
 369→vector center = chv("center");
 370→float radius = ch("radius");
 371→v@P = set(pos.x, pos.y, pos.z);
 372→@Cd = fit(length(pos), -3, 3, 0, 1);
 373→
 374→float d = length(@P);
 375→d *= ch('y_scale');
 376→// ...
 377→```
 378→
 379→Demonstrates multiple position manipulation techniques including exponential decay based on distance from origin, color assignment using fitted length values, sinusoidal wave displacement along Y-a....
 380→
 381→### Normal Updates After Displacement
 382→
 383→```vex
 384→float d = length(@P);
 385→d *= ch("v_scale");
 386→@P.y = sin(d);
 387→
 388→@P += @N * ch("push");
 389→```
 390→
 391→Demonstrates displacing geometry along normals using a sine wave pattern based on distance from origin.
 392→
 393→### Normal-based displacement
 394→
 395→```vex
 396→float d = length(@P);
 397→d *= ch('N_scale');
 398→d *= @Frame;
 399→@P.y = sin(d);
 400→
 401→@P += @N * ch('push');
 402→```
 403→
 404→This code demonstrates the difference between non-directional displacement (modifying only @P.y based on distance from origin) and proper normal-based displacement using @N.
 405→
 406→### Animated Normal Displacement
 407→
 408→```vex
 409→float d = length(@P);
 410→d *= ch('v_scale');
 411→d += @Time;
 412→@P += @N*sin(d);
 413→```
 414→
 415→Creates an animated wave displacement by calculating distance from origin, scaling it with a channel parameter, adding time for animation, and offsetting points along their normals using a sine wave.
 416→
 417→### Animated Wave Displacement with Controls
 418→
 419→```vex
 420→float d = length(@P);
 421→d *= ch('x_scale');
 422→d += @Frame;
 423→@P += @N * sin(d) * ch('wave_height');
 424→```
 425→
 426→Creates an animated wave displacement effect by calculating distance from origin, scaling it with a channel parameter, adding frame number for animation, and displacing points along their normals u....
 427→
 428→### Animated Wave Displacement
 429→
 430→```vex
 431→float d = length(@P);
 432→d *= ch("v_scale");
 433→d += @Frame;
 434→@P = @P * sin(d) * ch("wave_height");
 435→```
 436→
 437→Creates an animated radial wave displacement by calculating distance from origin, scaling it with parameters, adding frame-based animation, and applying a sine function modulated by wave height.
 438→
 439→### Animated Wave Deformation
 440→
 441→```vex
 442→float d = length(@P);
 443→d *= ch("y_scale");
 444→d += @Time;
 445→@P += @N*sin(d)*ch("wave_height");
 446→```
 447→
 448→Creates an animated wave deformation by calculating distance from origin, scaling it with a channel parameter, adding time for animation, and displacing points along their normals using a sine wave....
 449→
 450→### Fit function with channel parameters
 451→
 452→```vex
 453→float imin = ch('fit_in_min');
 454→float imax = ch('fit_in_max');
 455→float outMin = ch('fit_out_min');
 456→float outMax = ch('fit_out_max');
 457→float d = fit(d, imin, imax, outMin, outMax);
 458→@P.y = d;
 459→```
 460→
 461→Uses the fit() function to remap a value 'd' from one range to another, with input and output ranges controlled by channel parameters.
 462→
 463→### Ramp Parameter with Distance
 464→
 465→```vex
 466→float d = length(v@P);
 467→d *= ch('scale');
 468→@P.y = chramp('myramp', d);
 469→```
 470→
 471→This code calculates the distance from the origin using length(), scales it with a channel parameter, and then uses that scaled distance to sample a ramp parameter called 'myramp' which drives the ....
 472→
 473→### Ramp-Based Height Displacement
 474→
 475→```vex
 476→float d = length(v@P);
 477→@P.y = chramp('myramp', d);
 478→```
 479→
 480→Calculates the distance from the origin for each point and uses that distance to sample a ramp parameter named 'myramp', applying the result to the point's Y position.
 481→
 482→### Animated Radial Wave Pattern
 483→
 484→```vex
 485→float d = length(@P);
 486→d *= ch('scale');
 487→d -= @Time;
 488→d %= 1;
 489→@P.y = chramp('my-ramp', d);
 490→@P.y *= ch('height');
 491→```
 492→
 493→Creates an animated radial wave pattern by calculating distance from origin, scaling and offsetting by time, using modulo to create repeating rings, then applying a ramp to control vertical displac....
 494→
 495→### Stepped Ramp Channel Sampling
 496→
 497→```vex
 498→d = ch('pre_scale');
 499→d = chramp('my_stepped_ramp', d);
 500→d *= ch('post_scale');
 501→@P.y = d;
 502→```
 503→
 504→Demonstrates using a stepped ramp parameter to create striated or quantized values for geometry displacement.
 505→
 506→### Dot Product Basics
 507→
 508→```vex
 509→@Cd = @N.y;
 510→
 511→@Cd = @N.y;
 512→
 513→@Cd = dot(@N, {0,1,0});
 514→
 515→@Cd = dot(@N, chv('angle'));
 516→```
 517→
 518→Demonstrates multiple ways to calculate the dot product between a normal vector and an up vector (0,1,0).
 519→
 520→### Dot Product with Vectors and Channels
 521→
 522→```vex
 523→@Cd = dot(@N, {0,1,0});
 524→
 525→@Cd = @N.y;
 526→
 527→@Cd = dot(@N, {0,1,0});
 528→
 529→@Cd = dot(@N, chv('angle'));
 530→
 531→// ...
 532→```
 533→
 534→Demonstrates multiple approaches to computing dot products for lighting effects, starting with explicit vectors, then using component access, progressing to channel-driven vectors with chv(), and f....
 535→
 536→### Dot Product Direction Comparison
 537→
 538→```vex
 539→@Cd = dot(@N, chv('angle'));
 540→
 541→@Cd = dot(@N, (0,1,0));
 542→
 543→@Cd = dot(@N, chv('angle'));
 544→
 545→vector pos = point(1,"P",0);
 546→@Cd = dot(@N, pos);
 547→// ...
 548→```
 549→
 550→Demonstrates using dot product to compare normal direction against various reference vectors, including channel parameter vectors, hardcoded directions like (0,1,0), and point positions from other ....
 551→
 552→### Dot Product with Point Reference
 553→
 554→```vex
 555→vector pos = point(1, 'P', 0);
 556→@Cd = dot(@N, pos);
 557→
 558→vector pos = point(1, 'P', 0);
 559→@Cd = dot(@N, normalize(pos));
 560→
 561→@Cd = dot(@N, chv('angle'));
 562→
 563→// ...
 564→```
 565→
 566→Demonstrates using the point() function to read a position from a different input and calculate dot product with normals.
 567→
 568→### Reading Point Position from Second Input
 569→
 570→```vex
 571→vector pos = point(1, "P", @ptnum);
 572→@Cd = dot(chv("angle"), pos);
 573→```
 574→
 575→Reads the position of a point from the second input using the point() function, then uses a dot product between a channel vector parameter and that position to drive color.
 576→
 577→### Vector Addition Basics
 578→
 579→```vex
 580→vector a = chv('a');
 581→vector b = chv('b');
 582→@N = a+b;
 583→
 584→vector a = chv('a');
 585→@N += a;
 586→
 587→vector a = point(0,'P',@ptnum);
 588→// ...
 589→```
 590→
 591→Demonstrates basic vector addition using channel references and the tip-to-tail method.
 592→
 593→### Vector Addition Basics
 594→
 595→```vex
 596→vector a = chv('a');
 597→@i += a;
 598→
 599→vector b = point(0, "P", 0);
 600→vector c = point(1, "P", 0);
 601→```
 602→
 603→Demonstrates basic vector addition by adding a channel vector parameter to an attribute.
 604→
 605→### Vector Addition Basics
 606→
 607→```vex
 608→vector a = chv('a');
 609→@P += a;
 610→
 611→vector a = point(0, "P", @ptnum);
 612→vector b = point(1, "P", @ptnum);
 613→```
 614→
 615→Demonstrates basic vector addition by adding a channel-referenced vector to point positions.
 616→
 617→### Channel References and Point Queries
 618→
 619→```vex
 620→vector a = chv('a');
 621→@i += a;
 622→
 623→vector b = point(0,'P',0);
 624→vector c = point(1,'P',0);
 625→```
 626→
 627→Demonstrates reading a vector channel parameter using chv() and adding it to a custom attribute @i.
 628→
 629→### Vector Channel Parameters and Point Access
 630→
 631→```vex
 632→vector a = chv('a');
 633→@N += a;
 634→
 635→vector b = point(0, "P", 0);
 636→vector c = point(1, "P", 0);
 637→```
 638→
 639→Demonstrates creating a vector channel parameter using chv() to add to normal attributes, and shows how to access point positions from different inputs using the point() function.
 640→
 641→### Vector Subtraction Between Points
 642→
 643→```vex
 644→vector a = chv('A');
 645→@N += a;
 646→
 647→vector a = point(0, "P", 0);
 648→vector b = point(1, "P", 0);
 649→
 650→@N = b - a;
 651→
 652→// ...
 653→```
 654→
 655→Demonstrates vector subtraction by reading positions from two different point sources using the point() function.
 656→
 657→### Directional velocity from origin point
 658→
 659→```vex
 660→@v *= ch("scale");
 661→
 662→@v = -@N;
 663→@v *= ch("scale");
 664→
 665→vector origin = point(1, 'P', 0);
 666→@v = @P - origin;
 667→@v *= ch("scale");
 668→```
 669→
 670→Sets velocity vectors radiating outward from an origin point by computing the direction from the origin to each point position.
 671→
 672→### Vector Scaling and Direction
 673→
 674→```vex
 675→vector a = @P;
 676→vector b = point(1, @ptnum, "P");
 677→
 678→@N = b - a;
 679→
 680→@N *= chv('scalevec');
 681→
 682→vector origin = point(1, 'P', 0);
 683→// ...
 684→```
 685→
 686→Calculates a vector from current point to a corresponding point in second input, then scales it by a channel vector parameter.
 687→
 688→### Multiplying Normals by Vector
 689→
 690→```vex
 691→@N *= chv('scale_vec');
 692→```
 693→
 694→Multiplies the normal vector by a vector channel parameter to scale and flip normals non-uniformly.
 695→
 696→### Normalized Bounding Box Deformation
 697→
 698→```vex
 699→vector bbox = relpointbbox(0, @P);
 700→float i = chramp('inflate', bbox.y);
 701→@P += @N * i * ch('scale');
 702→
 703→vector bbox = relpointbbox(0, @P);
 704→@Cd = bbox.y;
 705→
 706→vector bbox = relpointbbox(0, @P);
 707→// ...
 708→```
 709→
 710→Uses relpointbbox() to get normalized bounding box coordinates (0-1 range) for each point's position, then applies deformation along the normal based on the Y-axis bbox value.
 711→
 712→### Relative Bounding Box Inflation
 713→
 714→```vex
 715→vector bbox = relbbox(0, @P);
 716→float i = chramp('inflate', bbox.y);
 717→@P += @N * i * ch('scale');
 718→```
 719→
 720→Uses relbbox() to get normalized position within bounding box (0-1 space), then samples a ramp parameter based on the Y component to drive a displacement along normals.
 721→
 722→### Setting Point Scale via Channel
 723→
 724→```vex
 725→@pscale = ch("pscale");
 726→```
 727→
 728→Sets the pscale attribute (which controls instance size in Copy to Points) by reading a channel parameter value.
 729→
 730→### Using @scale for non-uniform scaling
 731→
 732→```vex
 733→d = chf('frequency');
 734→d += @Time;
 735→d = fit(sin(d), -1, 1, ch('min'), ch('max'));
 736→
 737→// Different ways to set @scale vector:
 738→@scale = {1, 5, 2.5};
 739→
 740→@scale.x = 1;
 741→// ...
 742→```
 743→
 744→The @scale attribute allows non-uniform scaling on all three axes, unlike @pscale which scales uniformly.
 745→
 746→### Color and Scale from Distance Ramp
 747→
 748→```vex
 749→float min, max, d, t;
 750→min = ch('min');
 751→max = ch('max');
 752→t = chramp('ramp', @id);
 753→d = length(@P);
 754→d *= ch('frequency');
 755→d += t;
 756→d = fit(d, min, max, 0, 1);
 757→// ...
 758→```
 759→
 760→Calculates distance from origin, modulates it with frequency and ramp values, then uses fitted distance to drive both point scale and color through separate ramps.
 761→
 762→### Packed Geometry with Scale and Color
 763→
 764→```vex
 765→float min, max, d, t;
 766→min = ch('min');
 767→max = ch('max');
 768→t = fit01(v@pred);
 769→d = length(@P);
 770→d *= ch('frequency');
 771→d += t;
 772→d = fit(d, min, max, 0, 1);
 773→// ...
 774→```
 775→
 776→Creates packed geometry instances with non-uniform scaling based on distance from origin and a predecessor attribute.
 777→
 778→### Animated scaling with ground plane offset
 779→
 780→```vex
 781→float min, max, d, t;
